{"timestamp": "2026-08-09T18:33:50.233577", "data": {"url": "https://www.delish.com/food/a69209739/regular-vs-diet-soda-healthier/", "title": "Diet vs. Regular Soda: Which Is Healthier?", "content": "Mario Tama // Getty Images A dietitian compares diet and regular soda, outlining key benefits and drawbacks for each. Diet soda cuts sugar and calories, but raises questions about sweeteners and cravings. Regular soda delivers taste and nostalgia, but packs added sugar that can impact health. As a dietitian, I get asked a lot of questions. \"Is keto really the answer to everything?\" (Spoiler: no). \"Can I just eat pizza for dinner every night if I put spinach on it?\" (Nice try). And a common one: \"Which is healthier, diet soda or regular soda?\" This question is usually whispered, as if someone is confessing a deep, dark secret. People look at me, expecting a simple, definitive answer. They want a golden ticket to guilt-free fizz. However, the truth is that this debate is less like a clear-cut victory and more like one of those arguments with a loved one, where you both end up a little right and a little wrong. So, let's pop the top on this can of worms and get to the bottom of the great soda showdown. Grab your favorite bubbly beverage (for now), and let\u2019s dive in. The Deal With Diet Soda: Zero Calories, Full Confusion Ah, diet soda\u2014the siren song for anyone trying to cut back on sugar or calories. It promises all the fizzy satisfaction of its sugary cousin with none of the guilt. Dreamy, right? A calorie-free pass to deliciousness! But is it too good to be true? The Benefits The most obvious plus is the lack of sugar and calories. For someone managing weight or blood sugar, swapping a regular soda for a diet one can be a useful tool. It can satisfy a craving for something sweet and carbonated without contributing to your daily sugar intake. This simple switch can make a significant difference for people with diabetes or those working towards weight loss goals. It allows for a sense of normalcy and enjoyment without the metabolic consequences of a sugar-laden drink. The Pitfalls Here\u2019s where things get murky. Diet sodas derive their sweetness from artificial or low-calorie sweeteners, such as aspartame, sucralose, and acesulfame potassium (Ace-K). The research on these sweeteners is, to put it mildly, all over the place. Some studies suggest that these sweeteners are safe and have been used for decades. Other research has raised questions (and other agencies have raised questions with suggestions to avoid or limit certain ones), leaving many scratching their heads. Some research links high consumption of certain artificial sweeteners to changes in gut bacteria , which can affect everything from digestion to immune health; other preliminary data suggest some sweeteners don't impact the gut microbiota. There's also a behavioral angle: some people find that the intense sweetness of diet drinks can increase cravings for other sweet foods\u2014though other data suggests consuming something sweet, like diet soda, doesn't \"cause\" sugar cravings . So, does diet soda get the green light? The evidence is mixed, and it ultimately depends on how much you drink. A can here and there is unlikely to cause major issues, but relying on it as your primary source of hydration is not the best strategy. Actually, I'll be more firm: it's 1,000% not the best choice as your primary source of hydration. Add Delish as a preferred source in your Google search results. Use your Google account to add this to your settings. Add to Google settings Regular Soda: A Sweet Treat With A High Price Now, the OG: regular, full-sugar soda. It\u2019s the stuff of childhood parties and movie outings. That crisp, sweet rush is undeniably satisfying. There's nostalgia tied to it, and for many, it\u2019s pure comfort in a can. The Benefits From a purely nutritional standpoint, are there any benefits to regular soda? That's a tough one. You get quick energy from simple sugar, which can help in a true low-blood-sugar moment. Taste and enjoyment are the real draws for many, as it's made with sugar or high-fructose corn syrup, which our brains are hardwired to love. So, the primary benefit is simple: enjoyment\u2014oh, and for some, the caffeine is a bonus. The Pitfalls Here, the picture is clearer. The main drawback is the staggering sugar content. A single 12-ounce cola contains approximately 39 grams of sugar\u2014equivalent to about 10 teaspoons. The American Heart Association recommends no more than 25 grams (6 teaspoons) of added sugar per day for women and 36 grams (9 teaspoons) for men. One can of soda, and you\u2019ve already blown past that. High intake of liquid sugar is linked to an increased risk of weight gain, type 2 diabetes, heart disease, and dental cavities. Because the sugar is liquid, your body doesn't register fullness the same way it does with solid food, making \u201cempty calories\u201d easy to overdo. It\u2019s an easy way to consume \"empty calories\" that provide little to no nutritional value. And if you\u2019re avoiding diet soda for gut health concerns, know that regular soda\u2019s sugar doesn't support your gut microbiota either . So, Who Wins The Soda War? After all that, you might want a clear winner. The anticlimactic truth: neither takes the crown. No single food or drink\u2014soda included\u2014will make or break your overall diet. If most days you're eating colorful veggies, whole grains, lean proteins, and healthy fats, the occasional can of regular soda is hardly a nutritional catastrophe. What matters is the pattern of your choices over time, not one indulgence. That said, how much and how often you drink soda adds up. A rare treat is one thing; a daily habit is another. Making either diet or regular soda a habit can crowd out smarter choices and nudge your health the wrong way. So, which should you choose\u2014diet or regular? The answer depends on your goals. If you\u2019re cutting sugar or managing weight or blood sugar, diet soda has the edge: zero calories, no sugar rush. If artificial sweeteners don't sit well with you, an occasional regular soda, paired with a nutrient-rich diet, can fit just fine. (I said occasional , folks.) Bottom line? Moderation and context are everything. Soda, diet or regular, is best enjoyed like a slice of cake: for fun, not as a staple. Pick what works for you, savor it when you have it, and keep your foundation focused on nourishing foods and plenty of water. Your body will be glad you did. More Dietitian Insight Is It Unhealthy To Eat Dinner Late At Night? Dietitians Weigh In Greek vs. Regular Yogurt: A Dietitian Reveals The Healthier Choice 6 Foods & Drinks No One Over 30 Should Buy, According To A Dietitian 6 'Healthy' Foods That Really Aren't Very Healthy Dietitians Reveal The Healthiest Pizza You Can Eat\u2014And What To Avoid Is It Healthy To Eat Dessert Every Night? A Dietitian Explains", "lists": [["My Recipes", "Subscribe", "Membership Benefits", "Recipes", "Meals", "Holidays", "Food Trends", "News", "Kitchen Tips & Tools", "All Videos", "About Us", "Newsletter", "Follow"], ["Recipes", "Summer Recipes", "Occasions", "Kitchen Tips", "Food News & Trends", "Member Perks", "About Us", "My Bookmarks", "Newsletter"], ["", "Privacy Notice Terms Of Use"], ["Recipes", "Summer Recipes", "Occasions", "Kitchen Tips", "Food News & Trends", "Member Perks", "About Us", "More"], ["Food Trends", "Diet vs. Regular Soda: A Dietitian Reveals The Healthier Choice"], ["A dietitian compares diet and regular soda, outlining key benefits and drawbacks for each.", "Diet soda cuts sugar and calories, but raises questions about sweeteners and cravings.", "Regular soda delivers taste and nostalgia, but packs added sugar that can impact health."]], "tables": [], "code_blocks": [], "meta_description": "A registered dietitian breaks down diet vs. regular soda\u2014the pros, cons, and gut-health questions\u2014and explains when each can fit into a healthy routine.", "meta_keywords": "", "og_image": "https://hips.hearstapps.com/hmg-prod/images/two-liter-bottles-of-regular-and-diet-soda-are-seen-for-news-photo-1761932153.pjpeg?crop=0.881xw:0.691xh;0.0433xw,0.00503xh&resize=1200:*", "js_rendered": true, "js_message": "Page appears to be rendered by a JavaScript framework; content may be incomplete.", "success": true, "error": "", "truncated": false, "fetched_bytes": 593542, "total_bytes": 593542}}